.TH ESDM\-SERVER 8 "2026-10-16" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-server \- Entropy Source and DRNG Manager daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-server
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-server
is the central daemon of the Entropy Source and DRNG Manager (ESDM). It
collects data from all compiled\-in entropy sources, maintains the
deterministic random number generators seeded from them, and serves random
data to consumers.
.PP
Consumers reach the daemon through one of its frontends:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the RPC interface used by
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR esdm\-tool (1)
and the other ESDM frontends,
.IP \(bu 2
the character devices
.I /dev/random
and
.I /dev/urandom
provided by
.BR esdm\-cuse\-random (8)
and
.BR esdm\-cuse\-urandom (8),
.IP \(bu 2
the EGD protocol, if a socket is configured with
.B \-\-egd_socket
(see
.BR OPTIONS ).
.RE
.PP
The daemon must be started as root. It creates its listening sockets and its
status shared memory segment while privileged and then permanently drops to an
unprivileged user; see
.B \-\-username
and
.BR \-\-groupname .
Entropy sources that need privileges for their setup are initialized before
the privileges are dropped, and keep what they opened then: the TPM 2.0 entropy
source its device, the PKCS#11 entropy source its module. After a failure \-
an I/O error of the TPM, a token that fails or is removed \- they try again at
most every 5 seconds, on what they keep: the TPM device is opened anew only
where the privileges left still allow it, and a token is looked for among the
slots of the loaded module, which sees a token added later only if the module
reports one without being loaded again (SoftHSM does not). A device or module
that could not be opened at startup is not opened again once the privileges
are dropped.
.PP
On systemd installations the daemon is normally not started by hand but
through the shipped
.B esdm\-server.service
unit and its socket units.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version of the ESDM library the daemon was built against and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Increase the logging verbosity. May be given repeatedly, the count being the
verbosity level. Errors and warnings are logged at any level, the default
included; \-vvvv adds verbose messages, and each further \-v more debugging
output. Any use of this option implies
.BR \-\-foreground .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid " \fIFILE\fR"
Write the PID of the daemon to
.IR FILE .
The daemon keeps the file locked for as long as it runs, its shutdown included,
so another instance cannot start before it is gone. It writes the file
before it drops its privileges, and can then no longer remove it from a
directory only root may write to, such as
.IR /run .
So at shutdown it empties the file instead, which then names no process; the
next start reuses it, and systemd removes the file of a service configured with
.B PIDFile=
itself. With
.BR \-\-pid_namespace ,
the file names the supervisor, which keeps its privileges and removes the file
when it exits.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-username " \fIUSER\fR"
Unprivileged user to switch to after the privileged initialization. Defaults
to
.BR nobody .
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-groupname " \fIGROUP\fR"
Supplemental group to switch to after the privileged initialization. By
default no supplemental group is set.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-foreground
Do not detach from the terminal.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-force_irqes
Force the interrupt entropy source to be enabled in cases where the ESDM
would otherwise keep retrying to enable it.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-force_schedes
The same for the scheduler entropy source.
.TP
.B \-\-jent_block_disable
Disable the asynchronous block collection of the Jitter RNG entropy source.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-syslog
Log to syslog instead of standard output and standard error.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-raise_sched_priority
Raise the scheduling priority (lower the nice level) of the daemon.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-memlock
Lock all memory of the daemon so that it is never swapped out, and disable
core dumps. Keeping seed material out of swap and out of crash dumps is the
point of this option.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-small_memory
Reduce the memory footprint: 2 MiB thread stacks and a single DRNG node
instead of one per CPU node.
.TP
.B \-\-keep_ipc
Do not remove the RPC sockets, the status shared memory segment and the
semaphores on shutdown. This lets running CUSE clients survive a restart of
the daemon. Only the supervisor of
.B \-\-pid_namespace
removes them: without it, the daemon has dropped the privileges it would need
by then and leaves them in place, and the next start replaces them.
.TP
.B \-\-pid_namespace
Fork the daemon into an isolating PID namespace. The starting process remains
as its supervisor.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-egd_socket " \fIPATH\fR"
Additionally serve the EGD protocol on the UNIX domain socket
.IR PATH .
Disabled by default. The EGD protocol cannot ask for prediction resistance;
the socket serves it with commands of the ESDM's own, see
.BR "EGD PROTOCOL EXTENSIONS" .
.TP
.BR \-\-pkcs11_module " \fIPATH\fR"
Load the PKCS#11 provider module
.I PATH
for the PKCS#11 entropy source instead of the module the build was configured
with (meson option
.BR es_pkcs11_module_path ).
The module is loaded while the daemon still has its privileges, and not loaded
again once they are dropped (see
.BR DESCRIPTION ).
Refused by a build without the PKCS#11 entropy source (meson option
.BR es_pkcs11 ).
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_lib " \fIPATH\fR"
Load the plugin library
.I PATH
as external entropy source instead of the one the build was configured with
(meson option
.BR es_ext_lib_path ),
see
.B EXTERNAL ENTROPY SOURCES
below. Like all options of the external entropy sources, it may be given once
for each source of its kind the build has: the n-th occurrence configures the
n-th source.
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_lib_entropy_rate " \fIBITS\fR"
Credit a block of the plugin with at most
.I BITS
bits of entropy per 256 bits of data, 0 to 256, instead of the rate of the
build (meson option
.BR es_ext_lib_entropy_rate ,
256 by default).
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket " \fIPATH\fR"
Read the external entropy source a provider process serves on the UNIX domain
socket
.I PATH
instead of the one the build was configured with (meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_path ),
see
.B EXTERNAL ENTROPY SOURCES
below.
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate " \fIBITS\fR"
Credit a block of the provider with at most
.I BITS
bits of entropy per 256 bits of data, 0 to 256 (meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_entropy_rate ).
The default is 0: the data of a provider is not credited with entropy until a
rate is configured.
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket_uid " \fIUSER\fR"
Accept a provider running as
.I USER
\- a uid or a user name \- next to one running as root (meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_peer_uid ).
.TP
.BR \-\-es_jent_kernel_name " \fINAME\fR"
Read the kernel crypto API RNG
.I NAME
\- its name or driver name in
.I /proc/crypto
\- for the kernel Jitter RNG entropy source, instead of the one the build was
configured with (meson option
.BR es_jent_kernel_name ,
.B jitterentropy_rng
by default): e.g. that of a jitterentropy kernel module registering its RNG
under another name. Refused by a build without the source (meson option
.BR es_jent_kernel ).
.TP
.B \-\-no_dtls
Serve the RPC interfaces in the clear only: a client asking for DTLS is
refused, and goes on unencrypted on the same connection. Without DTLS in the
build, that is what the daemon does anyway. See
.B ENCRYPTION
below.
.SH EXTERNAL ENTROPY SOURCES
Noise sources that are not part of the ESDM are written against
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3)
as a plugin and handed to the daemon in one of two ways.
.PP
A plugin library given with
.B \-\-es_ext_lib
is loaded into the daemon and its initialization callback run while the daemon
still has its privileges \- a device the plugin reads is opened there. The
daemon has left the mount, cgroup and network namespaces of the host before,
so the callback reaches no network already: no TCP or UDP endpoint and no
abstract UNIX domain socket of the host, only a socket bound to a path.
Afterwards the plugin runs with the privileges and in the namespaces of the
daemon. A library that cannot be loaded, exports no plugin of the supported
version, or fails its initialization leaves the source switched off; the
reason is logged.
.PP
The daemon has as many plugin library sources and provider socket sources as
the build was configured with (meson options
.B es_ext_lib_num
and
.BR es_ext_socket_num ,
one of each by default, up to 16 each). Each is an entropy source of its own,
with its own plugin or provider, entropy rate, accepted user and state, credited
and failing on its own. The options of the external entropy sources configure
one source each time they are given, the n-th occurrence of an option the n-th
source of its kind, counted per option; an occurrence beyond the sources of the
build is refused. With one source of a kind, it is called
.B ExternalLib
or
.B ExternalSocket
in the status; with several, they are called
.BR ExternalLib0 ,
.B ExternalLib1
and so on. A plugin library serves one source: a second source given the plugin
another one uses refuses it. Several providers are served by several
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8)
processes, each on a socket of its own:
.PP
.nf
esdm\-es\-ext\-serve \-\-plugin /usr/lib/esdm/a.so \-\-socket /run/esdm\-es\-a.socket
esdm\-es\-ext\-serve \-\-plugin /usr/lib/esdm/b.so \-\-socket /run/esdm\-es\-b.socket
esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket /run/esdm\-es\-a.socket \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate 128 \\
	\-\-es_ext_socket /run/esdm\-es\-b.socket \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate 64
.fi
.PP
Each block is credited with the entropy the plugin claims for it, but with at
most what the configured rate allows. A plugin that fails to deliver, or claims
more entropy than the block can hold, is credited with nothing until it
delivers again; the daemon asks it again on its own, at most every 5 seconds,
to find out. A plugin is credited with nothing before its first self test,
which runs before the daemon seeds its DRNGs, and a plugin failing its self
test with nothing until a self test passes.
.PP
A provider given with
.B \-\-es_ext_socket
is a process of its own serving the source with the provider server of
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3),
such as
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8).
The daemon connects to it at start up and whenever the connection was lost,
at most every 5 seconds after a failed attempt, and sends one request at a time.
A connection the provider closed while it was idle \- the provider was
restarted, or made room for another client \- is made again at once.
It checks the credentials of the provider on every connection: anybody who can
create the socket file could otherwise feed the daemon with data credited as
entropy, so only a provider running as root, or as the user given with
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket_uid ,
is accepted. The daemon connects with its privileges dropped once a connection
was lost, so the socket has to let the user it runs as
.RB ( \-\-username )
connect. A provider that does not answer within 2 seconds, or answers what the
protocol does not allow, is disconnected and not connected to again for 5
seconds; one that is not connected, or failed, is credited with nothing until
it delivers again, which the daemon finds out on its own. The same entropy credit
applies as for a plugin, with the rate of
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate ;
a provider that is not there at start up has its first self test on the first
connection to it.
.PP
A plugin, or a provider, may report a status of its own, a JSON object: the
status of the daemon shows it as the
.B provider
member of the state of the source, a section of its own in the text form
.RB ( "esdm\-tool \-\-status" ).
It is asked for at most every 5 seconds, the last one shown in between, and
not while the source serves a request \- neither the status nor the seeding
waits for the other. It is left out when it is no well-formed JSON object of up
to 512 bytes, and while it is due but the source is busy. A provider that does
not know the request closes the connection on it; the daemon connects again at
once and does not ask again until the connection is lost. One that does not
answer it within 2 seconds is disconnected as for any request, and not asked
for its status for a minute.
.PP
A daemon built with DTLS (meson option
.BR dtls )
asks the provider to encrypt the connection with DTLS, and uses it unencrypted
if the provider cannot; the status of the source says which. The DTLS is
unauthenticated \- the credentials of the provider remain what it is trusted
for \- and keeps the data credited as entropy from being read off the channel.
.SH ENCRYPTION
A daemon built with DTLS (meson option
.BR dtls )
offers to encrypt its RPC connections, on both interfaces, unless
.B \-\-no_dtls
is given.
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
of such a build asks for it on every new connection, with a STARTDTLS request
in the clear, and the DTLS 1.2 handshake follows when the daemon agrees. The
DTLS is opportunistic: a client not asking for it, such as one of ESDM 1.2.3
or before, is served in the clear as before, and a client whose daemon does not
agree \- or does not know STARTDTLS, as ESDM 1.2.3 and before \- goes on in the
clear. The DTLS is unauthenticated as well: the daemon presents an ephemeral
self\-signed certificate the client does not verify, and what it trusts a
client for are its credentials on the UNIX domain socket, as without DTLS. It
keeps the random numbers and the data the clients contribute from being read
off the connections by whoever can observe them.
.PP
The DTLS context with its ephemeral key is set up at start up, before the
privileges are dropped; should that fail, the daemon logs it and serves in the
clear. A handshake costs the daemon a fraction of a millisecond, so a server
thread spends at most 10 ms per round of its event loop on handshakes and goes
on with the others in its next round, after the requests of the other
connections. An idle encrypted connection holds some 130 KiB of memory of the
TLS stack, against a few hundred bytes for one in the clear: an unprivileged
user is agreed to DTLS on at most 64 connections at a time, and a server thread
on at most 256; a client beyond is refused DTLS and served in the clear.
Processes running as root are exempt from the bound per user. With debug
logging, the daemon logs every connection switching to DTLS with its cipher
suite.
.SH EGD PROTOCOL EXTENSIONS
Next to the five standard commands of the EGD protocol, the EGD socket serves
commands of the ESDM's own. Their codes lie far from the standard ones, so that
no EGD client sends one by accident; an EGD daemon that does not know them
closes the connection, as it does for any unknown command. All numbers are big
endian, like those of EGD, and the wire format is described in
.IR esdm_egd_protocol.h .
.TP
.B 0xe0
Prediction resistant non\-blocking read: a request byte follows, answered like
the standard non\-blocking read 0x01, but by the prediction resistance DRNG.
.TP
.B 0xe1
Prediction resistant blocking read: a request byte follows, answered like the
standard blocking read 0x02, but by the prediction resistance DRNG \- the
answer waits for the entropy it is made of.
.TP
.B 0xe2
Read with the semantics of
.IR /dev/urandom :
a request byte follows, answered like 0x01 from the DRNG without waiting for
all DRNGs to be fully seeded. Before the ESDM is operational it hands out
nothing, so the answer is empty then.
.TP
.B 0xe3
State: a count byte, followed by that many 4 byte fields \- flags (bit 0
operational, bit 1 fully seeded), the entropy of the auxiliary pool in bits,
its size in bits, the write wakeup threshold and the maximum reseed interval in
seconds. Fields are only ever appended.
.TP
.BR 0xe4 ", " 0xe5
Status report as text and as JSON document: a 4 byte length, at most 65536,
followed by the report without a terminating NUL. A text report that does not
fit is sent as far as it got, a JSON document that does not fit is sent empty.
.PP
The following change the entropy accounting or the DRNGs. They are carried out
only for a peer connected with UID 0, as the server learns from the peer
credentials of the socket \- the same rule as for the entropy claim of the
standard write command. Each is answered with a 4 byte status, 0 or a negative
errno, \-EPERM for any other peer, whose connection stays usable. A client
that is gone when the command is processed still has it carried out, but not
answered.
.TP
.B 0xe6
Credit the 4 byte number of bits that follows to the auxiliary pool, capped
at its size (RNDADDTOENTCNT).
.TP
.B 0xe7
Set the entropy of the auxiliary pool to zero (RNDZAPENTCNT, RNDCLEARPOOL).
.TP
.B 0xe8
Reseed the DRNGs at their next use (RNDRESEEDCRNG).
.TP
.BR 0xe9 ", " 0xea
Set the write wakeup threshold in bits, respectively the maximum reseed
interval in seconds, to the 4 byte number that follows.
.PP
The prediction resistant reads and the status reports are served within the
budgets described under
.BR "CONNECTION LIMITS" .
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT ", " SIGQUIT ", " SIGHUP
Shut the daemon down cleanly.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Mark the ESDM as suspended in the status shared memory segment, so that
consumers waiting on it notice that the daemon is about to be suspended. This
is what
.BR esdm\-server\-signal\-helper (8)
sends before the system goes to sleep.
.SH CONNECTION LIMITS
All interfaces share the file descriptor limit of the daemon (the shipped
.B esdm\-server.service
sets
.BR LimitNOFILE=8192 ).
So that no single local user can exhaust it and lock every other consumer out,
the daemon bounds the connections of each unprivileged user. Processes running
as root are exempt. A user is not simply the UID of the connecting process: an
unprivileged user may map the UIDs of its subordinate range in a user
namespace and connect as each of them, and these count as the user's own. A
process in a user namespace other than the daemon's is counted as the owner of
the outermost user namespace between the two that is not owned by root, as far
as the kernel shows the daemon that namespace \- which, once the daemon
dropped its privileges, it does only for processes it may trace. Otherwise, a
UID within a range of
.I /etc/subuid
counts as the user owning the range, unless that is root or does not resolve
to a UID; changes of the file take effect within a second. Any other UID is a
user of its own, and so is each UID of a user namespace that root created and
filled itself, such as that of a container.
.TP
.B RPC interface
At most 16 concurrent connections per online CPU and user, counted over all
RPC connections of all server threads - a client process holds up to one
connection per online CPU. The bound is at least 512 and at most half of the
file descriptor limit of the daemon; it is logged at startup. A connection that has been idle for the period set by the
.B esdm\-server\-rpc\-idle\-timeout\-usec
build option (2 seconds by default) is closed;
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
reconnects transparently.
The unprivileged connections of all UIDs together leave 2560 descriptors, but
at most half of the limit, to the EGD and privileged interfaces. This bound is
split evenly over the server threads, at most 1024 connections each, and logged
at startup. A thread whose share is taken leaves new connections to the others.
Once all shares are taken, a thread does not refuse a new connection but
closes one of its idle ones \- the least recently used one of the user holding
the most connections in that thread. A connection with a request not read yet
is never closed for that, nor is one of the privileged interface. One with an
answer its client has not read yet is closed only when no idle one is left; the
client still reads that answer.
.TP
.B EGD interface
Served by one worker thread per online CPU, at most four, each serving the
clients it accepted. At most 2048 concurrent connections in total and 256 per
user. A connection
that has received no command and owes no response for 60 seconds is closed
only when its slot is wanted: at most 256 slots are left, an accept ran out of
descriptors within that time, or its user was refused a connection and is still
at its limit. While slots are short, only the idle connections of the users
holding the most connections are closed, never those of root. Once all slots
are taken, a new
connection is not left waiting but a connection is closed to make room for it
\- one of a user holding the most connections of all, preferably of the user
of the new connection, the least recently active one with nothing owed to its
client if there is one, and never one of root or of a user holding fewer. A
server thread that serves no connection of such a user leaves the new
connection to the others, or turns it away.
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3)
reconnects transparently, which a client confined by seccomp after it connected
may not survive.
A worker thread spends at most 25 ms per round of its event loop on generating
prediction resistant data for the prediction resistant read commands, one
generation per connection, so that their clients cannot hold up the others of
that thread; a request beyond that waits for the next round, which follows at
once. Likewise, it renders one status report per connection and round.
.PP
With DTLS, an unprivileged user is served encrypted on at most 64 of these
connections at a time; see
.BR ENCRYPTION .
.PP
A connection beyond the limit of its user is accepted and closed at once. The
daemon logs a warning when a user reaches its limit, and again only after the
user has dropped below its limit in between. Apart from the scaling of the RPC bound, these limits are compile\-time
constants.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/esdm\-rpc\-unpriv.socket
Socket of the unprivileged RPC interface. Its directory is set by the
.B esdm\-server\-rpc\-path\-unprivileged
build option.
.TP
.I /run/esdm\-rpc\-priv.socket
Socket of the privileged RPC interface. Its directory is set by the
.B esdm\-server\-rpc\-path\-privileged
build option.
.TP
.I /run/esdm\-egd.socket
Default socket of the EGD interface, when the shipped systemd socket unit is
used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B esdm\-server
exits with a non\-zero status when it cannot initialize the ESDM or the
requested interfaces.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-tool (1),
.BR esdm\-cuse\-random (8),
.BR esdm\-cuse\-urandom (8),
.BR esdm\-proc (8),
.BR esdm\-kernel\-seeder (8),
.BR esdm\-server\-signal\-helper (8),
.BR libesdm (3),
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
